    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "MYC, RAS, or BCR-ABL oncogenes are responsible for causing at least 70% of all lymphomas. Each of these oncogenes individually, and through interactions with one another, regulates different pathways involved in cellular metabolism and can lead to increased cell proliferation causing tumor formation. Many studies have shown that glucose and glutamine metabolisms are implicated in tumorigenesis, making some lymphomas susceptible to metabolic inhibition. However, lipid metabolism in lymphomas remains poorly understood. Preliminary analysis of the MYC protein’s binding to metabolic genes shows a temporal progression from glucose to glutamine to lipid transcriptional regulation, spurring interest in the role of lipogenesis in lymphoma development and growth. In this study, we show via RNA-seq, Microarray and RT-qPCR functional validation that although MYC, RAS, and BRC-ABL differentially regulate glucose and glutamine metabolic genes, they all converge and upregulate lipid metabolic genes in MYC, RAS, and BCR-ABL lymphoma models. Moreover, inhibition of the lipid synthesis pathway by a drug, TOFA, induces a dose-dependent decrease of proliferation in all three oncogene-driven lymphomas, providing the potential for the development of a novel class of drugs targeting transcription in the treatment of lymphomas.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
